OpenWrt Fork Workflow
=====================

Purpose
-------
This document describes the recommended Git workflow for maintaining a fork of
the OpenWrt repository while keeping it synchronized with upstream development.

This approach keeps the fork clean and makes upgrading OpenWrt versions easy.

Repository Structure
--------------------

GitHub fork:
    https://github.com/<YOURUSER>/openwrt

Remotes:

    origin   -> your GitHub fork
    upstream -> official OpenWrt repository

Branches:

    main                upstream development branch
    openwrt-25.12       upstream release branch
    rds-25.12           local working branch for custom modifications


Initial Setup
-------------

1. Fork the OpenWrt repository on GitHub

    https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt


2. Clone your fork

    git clone git@github.com:<YOURUSER>/openwrt.git
    cd openwrt


3. Add the upstream OpenWrt repository

    git remote add upstream https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt.git


4. Fetch upstream branches

    git fetch upstream


Create a Local Tracking Branch
------------------------------

Example for OpenWrt 25.12:

    git checkout -b openwrt-25.12 upstream/openwrt-25.12


Push this branch to your fork:

    git push origin openwrt-25.12


Create Your Working Branch
--------------------------

Do NOT modify the upstream tracking branch directly.

Instead create a working branch:

    git checkout -b rds-25.12 openwrt-25.12


All custom work should occur in this branch.


Updating to a New Upstream Release
-----------------------------------

When a new upstream release arrives (e.g. 25.12.1), follow these steps in
order. Do not skip the pre-flight checks -- they prevent nasty surprises
during the rebase.


Step 1: Pre-flight Checks
..........................

    # Verify remotes are correct.
    # origin  -> your GitHub fork
    # upstream -> official OpenWrt repo

    git remote -v

    # Make sure the working tree is clean.
    # If you have uncommitted changes, either commit them or stash them
    # before proceeding:
    #
    #   git stash            (to stash)
    #   git stash pop        (to restore later)

    git status

    # Show branch tracking info.
    # openwrt-25.12 should track upstream/openwrt-25.12.
    # rds-25.12 should show how many commits ahead it is.

    git branch -vv

    # List the commits that are unique to your working branch.
    # These are the commits that will be replayed during the rebase.
    # Verify this matches what you expect -- no missing or extra commits.

    git log --oneline openwrt-25.12..rds-25.12

    # Verify that your working branch forked from the tip of the
    # tracking branch. The output hash should match the tip of
    # openwrt-25.12 shown in "git branch -v".

    git merge-base rds-25.12 openwrt-25.12

    # Check that your local tracking branch is in sync with origin.
    # Both commands should produce no output.

    git fetch origin
    git log --oneline origin/openwrt-25.12..openwrt-25.12
    git log --oneline openwrt-25.12..origin/openwrt-25.12


Step 2: Clean Up Commits (Optional)
....................................

If you have commits that should be squashed (e.g. a typo fix followed by
the real fix), clean them up now with an interactive rebase:

    git checkout rds-25.12
    git rebase -i openwrt-25.12

In the editor, use these commands to combine commits:

    pick   = keep the commit as-is
    fixup  = squash into the commit above, keep the above message
    fixup -C = squash into the commit above, keep THIS commit message
    squash = squash into the commit above, open editor to combine messages

Example -- merging a typo fix into the original:

    pick e15aa42 Update feeds.conf to include RDS feeeds
    fixup -C 1099d77 update feeds.conf to include RDS feeds

This produces a single commit with the corrected message.


Step 3: Stash Uncommitted Changes
..................................

If "git status" showed modified or staged files, stash them now:

    git stash

Skip this step if the working tree is already clean.


Step 4: Update the Tracking Branch
...................................

    git checkout openwrt-25.12
    git fetch upstream --tags
    git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12


Step 5: Update Main (Optional, Good Hygiene)
.............................................

    git checkout main
    git pull --rebase upstream main


Step 6: Check for Upstream-Merged Commits
.........................................

If your working branch contains commits from upstream PRs that may have
been merged into the release, check for them now:

    git log --oneline openwrt-25.12 | grep -i "<search term>"

Example:

    git log --oneline openwrt-25.12 | grep -i "AsiaRF AP7988"

If the commit appears in the upstream branch, git will automatically
skip it during the rebase (or produce an empty commit that you can
skip with "git rebase --skip").


Step 7: Rebase Your Working Branch
...................................

    git checkout rds-25.12
    git rebase openwrt-25.12

This replays your custom commits on top of the updated upstream branch.

If you hit conflicts:

    # Edit the conflicting files to resolve
    git add <resolved-files>
    git rebase --continue

If an upstream-merged commit produces an empty result:

    git rebase --skip

To abandon the rebase entirely and return to the previous state:

    git rebase --abort


Step 8: Push Everything
.......................

    # Tracking branches are fast-forwards, no force needed.
    git push origin openwrt-25.12
    git push origin main

    # Working branch history was rewritten by the rebase.
    # --force-with-lease is required but safer than --force.
    git push origin rds-25.12 --force-with-lease


Step 9: Restore Stashed Changes
................................

If you stashed changes in Step 3:

    git stash pop


Step 10: Verify
...............

    # Confirm your commits are cleanly on top of the new upstream.
    git log --oneline openwrt-25.12..rds-25.12

    # Confirm the merge base is now the new tip of openwrt-25.12.
    git merge-base rds-25.12 openwrt-25.12


Deleting Stale PR Branches
---------------------------

If you applied an upstream PR as a local branch and it has since been
merged into your working branch or upstream, you can safely delete it.

First confirm that the changes exist in your working branch:

    git log --oneline <pr-branch>..rds-25.12 -- <relevant/path>

If the commits appear in rds-25.12, delete the branch:

    git branch -D <pr-branch>

Use -D (capital) for branches that have no tracking remote and were
never merged in the git sense.


Using Rebase Instead of Merge
-----------------------------

Always use rebase when syncing with upstream.

Correct:

    git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12

Avoid:

    git merge upstream/openwrt-25.12

Rebasing keeps the commit history clean and avoids unnecessary merge commits.


Optional Git Alias
------------------

You can simplify upstream syncing by adding this alias to ~/.gitconfig:

    [alias]
    sync = !"git fetch upstream && git rebase upstream/$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"

After adding this alias you can update your branch with:

    git sync

Note: This alias only works on branches that have a matching upstream
ref name (e.g. openwrt-25.12, main). It will NOT work on your working
branch (rds-25.12) because upstream/rds-25.12 does not exist.


Recommended Patch Strategy
--------------------------

For large OpenWrt customizations it is often easier to maintain changes as
patches.

Example structure:

    patches/
        001-feature.patch
        002-driver.patch
        003-package.patch


Apply patches with:

    git am patches/*

Generate patches from your working branch:

    git format-patch openwrt-25.12 -o patches/

This approach makes upgrading between OpenWrt versions significantly easier.


Recommended Vendor Branch Layout
--------------------------------

    openwrt
    ├── main
    ├── openwrt-25.12
    └── rds-25.12

    openwrt-25.12
        Pure upstream tracking branch. Never commit directly.

    rds-25.12
        RDS/PredictWind DataHub firmware modifications.
        Always rebased on top of openwrt-25.12.


Quick Reference: Update Checklist
----------------------------------

    [ ] git status                          # clean working tree?
    [ ] git log --oneline openwrt-25.12..rds-25.12   # expected commits?
    [ ] git stash                           # if needed
    [ ] git checkout openwrt-25.12
    [ ] git fetch upstream --tags
    [ ] git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12
    [ ] git checkout rds-25.12
    [ ] git rebase openwrt-25.12
    [ ] git push origin openwrt-25.12
    [ ] git push origin rds-25.12 --force-with-lease
    [ ] git stash pop                       # if stashed
    [ ] git log --oneline openwrt-25.12..rds-25.12   # verify


Notes
-----

Do not modify upstream tracking branches directly.

Always develop on a separate working branch.

Rebasing keeps the history clean and makes long-term maintenance much easier.

When resolving rebase conflicts, take time to understand both sides.
If unsure, "git rebase --abort" returns to the pre-rebase state with
no damage done.
